1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,947
Typing a long SSH command every time is tedious.

2
00:00:03,947 --> 00:00:12,520
Give each Pi a short nickname in a small configuration file, and then you simply type SSH, followed by the nickname.

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:18,083
To send a file from your laptop to the Pi, use SCP.

4
00:00:18,083 --> 00:00:21,580
Write the source first, then the destination:

5
00:00:21,580 --> 00:00:26,010
the user name, the Pi, a colon, and the folder on the Pi.

6
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:30,405
To fetch a file from the Pi, swap the order.

7
00:00:30,405 --> 00:00:34,693
The file on the Pi comes first, then the folder on your laptop.

8
00:00:34,693 --> 00:00:37,280
A single dot means the current folder.

9
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:45,404
For a full graphical desktop, enable VNC on the Pi, open a VNC viewer on your laptop, and sign in.

10
00:00:45,404 --> 00:00:51,510
You then see the Pi's desktop in a window, and you can use your mouse and keyboard on it.

11
00:00:53,110 --> 00:00:53,643
Remember:

12
00:00:53,643 --> 00:01:03,060
all of these need your laptop and the Pi to reach each other, either on the same network, or through a remote-access service when they are in different places.
